-- Typechecks cleanly. Each rule answers a list of n numbers whose end raises:
-- a REFUSE or a division by zero, either as one more element or as the rest
-- of the list. A directive reads a list answer only so deep, so it reaches
-- that end for the smaller n below and not for the larger. `l4 run` and
-- `l4 batch` must reach it for the same n.
@export the numbers, then a refusal as one more element
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A LIST OF NUMBER
`refusal in the list` n MEANS
  IF n EQUALS 0 THEN LIST (REFUSE "deep") ELSE n FOLLOWED BY `refusal in the list` (n MINUS 1)

@export the numbers, then a refusal as the rest of the list
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A LIST OF NUMBER
`refusal as the rest` n MEANS
  IF n EQUALS 0 THEN REFUSE "deep" ELSE n FOLLOWED BY `refusal as the rest` (n MINUS 1)

@export the numbers, then a division by zero as one more element
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A LIST OF NUMBER
`division in the list` n MEANS
  IF n EQUALS 0 THEN LIST (1 DIVIDED BY 0) ELSE n FOLLOWED BY `division in the list` (n MINUS 1)

@export the numbers, then a division by zero as the rest of the list
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A LIST OF NUMBER
`division as the rest` n MEANS
  IF n EQUALS 0 THEN (IF 1 DIVIDED BY 0 EQUALS 0 THEN EMPTY ELSE EMPTY) ELSE n FOLLOWED BY `division as the rest` (n MINUS 1)

#EVAL `refusal in the list` 200
#EVAL `refusal in the list` 201
#EVAL `refusal in the list` 202
#EVAL `refusal as the rest` 200
#EVAL `refusal as the rest` 201
#EVAL `refusal as the rest` 202
#EVAL `division in the list` 200
#EVAL `division in the list` 201
#EVAL `division in the list` 202
#EVAL `division as the rest` 200
#EVAL `division as the rest` 201
#EVAL `division as the rest` 202
